{
  "dom_id": "step-q49319",
  "name": "Question 13",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041851/assets/aad42b26d165789da802c0f507e3445976f3269b4c77e8b47201fb043b08f1f6.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the velocity of an object moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis. At time $t=0$ the particle is located at the position $x=6.$ What is the position of the particle at time $t=3?$",
  "worked_solution": "The position of the particle at $t=3$ is equal to its position at $t=0$ plus the total displacement over $t\\in [0,3].$ Therefore, we can calculate it as follows:\n\n$$\nx(3)=x(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{3}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nWe are told that $x(0)=6$, and we can compute the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{3}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t$ by finding the signed area under the graph from $t=0$ to $t=3.$ Computing the signed area, we get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{3}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t=5.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nx(3)=6+5=11\n$$\n\nSo the position of the particle at $t=3$ is $x=11.$",
